From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xe Chromium Patches <luke@agent54.org>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 01:03:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 49/50] Experiment (H): per-display-frame copy budget with priorities for keyed copies (kKeyedCopyBudget)

API: createTextureSource({priority: 'high' | 'low'}) (default 'high') and
GuestFrameTextureSource.setPriority(); the browser marks its copy requests
(new CopyOutputRequest::low_priority, serialized) and
GuestFrameTextureSourceHost gains SetLowPriority().

Viz: FrameSinkManagerImpl::AdmitKeyedCopy() keeps one budget per display
frame (identified by the expected display time; kKeyedCopyBudget 'units',
default 8 units of 640x360 source pixels). High-priority copies are always
admitted and charged; a low-priority copy is admitted only while budget
remains, or after waiting 'max_wait_frames' (6) frames (aging). In
CompositorFrameSinkSupport a parked low-priority request that is not
admitted stays parked with the sink's latest content as its dirty state; it
is retried on every drawn frame (OnSurfaceWillDraw), also without new
damage, and an admitted retry forces a redraw so the copy is taken at the
next aggregation. Cancellation and cleanup forget the per-key state. Disabled by default.
Not
done: feeding sustained deferral back into the guest's BeginFrame interval.

Measured (N = 64 synthetic animating guests, first 8 high priority,
Release, 2 runs each): budget off / on - scene 24.5 / 27.9 fps, p99 frame
85 / 78 ms, high group 23.9 / 26.2 fps, low group 23.9 / 25.2 fps, focus
latency 85 / 73 ms, max card age high 82 / 68 ms, low 82 / 142 ms. The
GPU main thread stays at 98%: guest raster, not copies, is the load, so a
copy budget alone does not protect the scene.
---
